    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d21">

                <pb facs="0155" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg31"/>

                <head>No. 21.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kasson to Mr. Evarts</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-12-31"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 31, 1880</date>.
                        (Received January 15, 1881.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 402.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In connection with the question of equality
                    of rights secured to the Hebrew and other races in Servia, in accordance with
                    the Berlin treaty, it will be of interest to you to know the recent step in
                    advance taken by the new government at Belgrade.</p>
                <p>As I have formerly advised you, in addition to the members of the Skuptchina
                    elected by the people, the government itself appoints forty members. It is now
                    stated that the Prince has just signed the decree, naming this list, which
                    instead of embracing, as formerly, government officials only, now contains the
                    names of persons of the various professions of the country, and includes one
                    Hebrew of Belgrade, and one Turk of Nisch.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN A. KASSON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
